  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Fixed:\n- When two observances of a calendar's VTIMEZONE begin at the same instant, GetUtcOffset now uses the last of them, as GetTransitions does, so ToUtc and ToLocal agree with the transitions.\n- A VTIMEZONE RDATE onset no longer takes effect up to a second early for an instant with a fraction of a second.\n- Setting IcalAlarm.TriggerDateTime, or TriggerDuration on an alarm with an absolute trigger, now keeps the TRIGGER's other parameters and its position instead of dropping them and moving it to the end.\n- IcalContentLineWriter.WriteLineAsync returns I/O errors in its task instead of throwing them.\n- `IcalTimeZoneResolver.Calendar.TryResolve` returns `false` for an empty time zone identifier instead of throwing `ArgumentException`.\n- IcalContentLine.Parameters and IcalContentLineParameter.Values can no longer be cast to arrays and changed.\n- The file-path constructors of `IcalReader`, `IcalWriter`, `IcalContentLineReader`, `IcalContentLineWriter`, `XcalReader` and `XcalWriter` open the file with `FileOptions.Asynchronous`, so their asynchronous methods no longer do synchronous file I/O.\n- IcalTimeZoneResolver.Calendar rules give the right offset before a VTIMEZONE's first onset.\n- XcalReader created from a file path closes the file when disposed before the first read.\n- On .NET Framework, `IcalContentLineWriter.DisposeAsync()` and `XcalWriter.DisposeAsync()` no longer call the synchronous `Flush()` of a stream or text writer you supplied.\n\nChanged:\n- Resolving times through a calendar's own VTIMEZONE, the default resolver, is faster and allocates less, because observance onsets are now expanded once per year and reused.\n- A VTIMEZONE with an empty `TZID` is reported as `InvalidValue`, so readers reject it and writers refuse it unless `AllowInvalidValues` is set.",
